luckily it was nothing,

00:07:57.963 --> 00:07:58.960
and then they did more tests to me,

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:01.760
it was really fast though,
it was like two hours.

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:03.256
I didn't have to wait or anything.

00:08:03.256 --> 00:08:05.670
- As someone from the
UK, it's really weird,

00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:07.920
going to any other healthcare system,

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:09.990
and just being able to get your results,

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:11.920
and going and seeing
the doctor the same day.

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:13.800
- Having an x-ray on the day of,

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:15.280
and getting the results on the day,

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:16.762
that doesn't happen in the UK.

00:08:16.762 --> 00:08:17.595
- Oh, really?

00:08:17.595 --> 00:08:18.830
- I remember, I thought I broke my leg,

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:20.300
and they made me make an appointment

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:22.850
for like a week and a
half to get the x-ray.

00:08:22.850 --> 00:08:23.683
- What?

00:08:23.683 --> 00:08:24.516
- And I thought, what?

00:08:24.516 --> 00:08:25.860
Bro my leg is like fucked now,

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:26.987
what do you mean- (laughing)

00:08:26.987 --> 00:08:28.330
I need the x-ray now.

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:29.781
- What'd you expect me to do with this

00:08:29.781 --> 00:08:30.892
for a whole week and a half, yeah?

00:08:30.892 --> 00:08:32.410
- I guess, 'cause it's free, right?

00:08:32.410 --> 00:08:33.247
Everyone gets it.

00:08:33.247 --> 00:08:34.080
- Yeah, yeah.
- So, I mean,

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:35.227
I guess you can argue,
which is better, right?

00:08:35.227 --> 00:08:36.538
- I mean, you have to
pay for it here, right?

00:08:36.538 --> 00:08:38.580
- You pay 1/3 right?

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:40.850
- It's very cheap.

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:45.850
- Well, I paid 1/3, and I
paid $77 for a COVID test,

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:49.281
a blood test and a CT, a CAT scan.

00:08:49.281 --> 00:08:52.580
- Yeah, Americans are probably
like losing their shit.

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:53.700
- They're like what the fuck?

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:55.658
- Like a CT scan, for $77?
- Yeah.

00:08:55.658 --> 00:08:57.790
- What the fuck?

00:08:57.790 --> 00:08:59.180
- Well, I got the breakdown,

00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:02.120
and the COVID test was 600 yen so like $5.

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:04.039
- Like five bucks, yeah.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:09:04.039 --> 00:09:05.057
It was like nothing.

00:09:05.057 --> 00:09:07.910
- 'Cause I remember I had to
take Aki to the hospital once,

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:09.020
this was like a year ago.

00:09:09.020 --> 00:09:10.009
- Right.

00:09:10.009 --> 00:09:13.479
- I think she squirted
shampoo directly into her eye.

00:09:13.479 --> 00:09:14.920
- Oh, yeah, I remember that.

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:16.010
- And she was like, I can't see,

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:16.970
I think I've gone blind.

00:09:16.970 --> 00:09:18.390
So I had to call up the hospital,

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:20.576
obviously get an ambulance
over and everything.

00:09:20.576 --> 00:09:21.409
- Yeah.

00:09:21.409 --> 00:09:23.030
- And the entire ride,
such an American mindset,

00:09:23.030 --> 00:09:24.857
the entire ride to the
hospital she's like,

00:09:24.857 --> 00:09:27.788
"Oh my God, this is gonna
be so expensive, isn't it?

00:09:27.788 --> 00:09:29.253
Like this thing is gonna be so..."

00:09:29.253 --> 00:09:30.880
And I'm just like, don't worry about it.

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:31.713
We'll just fix your eye up, whatever.

00:09:31.713 --> 00:09:33.463
- Yeah.
- We get to the hospital,

00:09:33.463 --> 00:09:34.790
it's like 2:00 am.
- Yeah.

00:09:34.790 --> 00:09:35.623
- We get to the hospital,

00:09:35.623 --> 00:09:37.350
doctor does a quick little
check and she's like,

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:39.530
okay, you're all good,
you're not blind or anything,

00:09:39.530 --> 00:09:41.750
it's just really red,
it's really burnt out.

00:09:41.750 --> 00:09:45.020
we wash it out for you,
here, take some meds,

00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:45.853
and you'll be all good.

00:09:45.853 --> 00:09:47.146
- Yeah.

00:09:47.146 --> 00:09:49.420
- And then in Japanese
hospitals, there's like a kiosk,

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:51.401
that you have to go to like pay for it.

00:09:51.401 --> 00:09:52.234
- Yeah.

00:09:52.234 --> 00:09:55.103
- Right, so we go to the kiosk
with like this little receipt

00:09:55.103 --> 00:09:56.680
and we're just like, oh my God,

00:09:56.680 --> 00:09:59.430
this is gonna be like couple
100 bucks at least, right?

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:01.580
'Cause she's thinking in
the American mentality.

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:03.401
- [Garnt] American mentality.
- [Connor] Yeah?

00:10:03.401 --> 00:10:04.650
- And I had never really gone
to a Japanese hospital before.

00:10:04.650 --> 00:10:07.050
So I had no idea either, I was
like, oh shit it's the same?

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:08.847
as like, if she says it's
like a couple 100 bucks,

00:10:08.847 --> 00:10:11.240
then I guess I'm prepared
to spend a couple 100 bucks.

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:12.701
Dude, it was like $12.

00:10:12.701 --> 00:10:13.790
(laughing)

00:10:13.790 --> 00:10:14.867
And we looked at it and she's like,

00:10:14.867 --> 00:10:17.334
"Wait, ambulances are free?"

00:10:17.334 --> 00:10:18.167
(laughing)

00:10:18.167 --> 00:10:20.730
Because in America you
have to pay like two, $300,

00:10:20.730 --> 00:10:22.010
to get a fucking ambulance.

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:22.843
- Yeah.
- I think it's more,

00:10:22.843 --> 00:10:23.676
I think it's like 1,000 plus.

00:10:23.676 --> 00:10:24.513
- It's something like that.

00:10:24.513 --> 00:10:26.250
Like I've heard stories of like

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:27.360
a couple of my American friends,

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:29.440
who broke their arm for example,

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:30.670
and they're like call an Uber.

00:10:30.670 --> 00:10:31.503
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:10:31.503 --> 00:10:33.650
- It was because we're not
paying for a fucking ambulance.

00:10:33.650 --> 00:10:34.483
- Yeah.

00:10:34.483 --> 00:10:35.317
- So we're just gonna call an Uber,

00:10:35.317 --> 00:10:36.220
with like your arm hanging off a thread.

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:37.341
- Yeah.

00:10:37.341 --> 00:10:39.680
- And it's tragic 'cause,
I think I remember,

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:41.760
Aki wanted to get an allergy test,

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:44.650
in, not in Australia, sorry, in America,

00:10:44.650 --> 00:10:45.823
and it was like $800.

00:10:45.823 --> 00:10:48.050
- Oh, Jesus.
- For an allergy test.

00:10:48.050 --> 00:10:49.890
- Yeah.
- Here you'd pay like $10

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:50.848
for an allergy test.

00:10:50.848 --> 00:10:52.452
- Yeah.
- And I guess in the UK,

00:10:52.452 --> 00:10:55.270
do you guys have free health care?

00:10:55.270 --> 00:10:56.620
- Yeah, we have free health care.

00:10:56.620 --> 00:10:57.730
- Yeah.

00:10:57.730 --> 00:10:59.340
- I think doctors won't give
you stuff like that there,

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:00.660
unless they think you need it though.

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:02.519
Like, so it's kind of hard
to get stuff like that.

00:11:02.519 --> 00:11:03.352
- Oh, all right.

00:11:03.352 --> 00:11:04.185
'Cause in Australia,

00:11:04.185 --> 00:11:05.760
you can go get an allergy
test whenever for free.

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:06.980
- Yeah, maybe, I don't know,

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:08.385
I've never done one I can't comment.

00:11:08.385 --> 00:11:11.230
- Some very specific
medicines you get for free,

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:12.907
others you kind of have to pay for,

00:11:12.907 --> 00:11:16.670
I feel like with UK, it's very specific

00:11:16.670 --> 00:11:19.290
on what illness you have,

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:21.640
because sometimes you have like
this really common illness,

00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:23.090
they just tell you to just go home,

00:11:23.090 --> 00:11:24.413
and sleep it off basically.

00:11:24.413 --> 00:11:25.246
(laughing)

00:11:25.246 --> 00:11:26.137
- Yeah, UK doctors a lot of the time,

00:11:26.137 --> 00:11:28.128
are very reluctant to like do anything.

00:11:28.128 --> 00:11:31.310
I mean, at least the doctors I've had.

00:11:31.310 --> 00:11:33.353
- I mean, if you just go to a
general practitioner, right?

00:11:33.353 --> 00:11:35.207
Like to get a checkup,
it's usually free, right?

00:11:35.207 --> 00:11:36.672
- Yeah.
- Whereas the only other,

00:11:36.672 --> 00:11:39.505
I guess, experience I've
had with health care,

00:11:39.505 --> 00:11:41.450
outside of the UK is Thailand

00:11:41.450 --> 00:11:42.801
and normally it's pretty,

00:11:42.801 --> 00:11:45.450
it's about the same price as it is here,

00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:46.900
in terms of medicine

00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:48.730
and going to see the
doctors and everything,

00:11:48.730 --> 00:11:51.222
and what I found is that doctors here,

00:11:51.222 --> 00:11:54.190
in Asia at least, are way
more willing to give off,

00:11:54.190 --> 00:11:56.576
some kind of medicine for
whatever illness you have.

00:11:56.576 --> 00:11:58.203
- Yeah, definitely.
- I guess there's an argument

00:11:58.203 --> 00:11:59.036
to make that also could
be dangerous though,

00:11:59.036 --> 00:12:00.570
just giving off medicine constantly.

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:02.400
I mean that's why there's
an opioid epidemic

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:03.240
right now in America.

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:04.850
- Yeah.
- Over prescribing.

00:12:04.850 --> 00:12:08.930
But, I mean, healthcare is a loaded topic.

00:12:08.930 --> 00:12:10.146
- That's a loaded topic,

00:12:10.146 --> 00:12:12.720
that I certainly do not know enough about,

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:13.553
to contribute to the conversation.

00:12:13.553 --> 00:12:16.970
- Either way, I had a good
time, my doctors were very nice,

00:12:16.970 --> 00:12:19.598
except they scared the
shit out of me that was-

00:12:19.598 --> 00:12:21.018
- Literally scared the shit out of you.

00:12:21.018 --> 00:12:22.090
(laughing)

00:12:22.090 --> 00:12:22.923
- I'm still shitting.
- Several times.

00:12:22.923 --> 00:12:24.024
- Actually, and I think
that what's good is,

00:12:24.024 --> 00:12:27.450
there was a pharmacy
right around the corner

00:12:27.450 --> 00:12:29.237
and they obviously knew I
couldn't speak Japanese,

00:12:29.237 --> 00:12:31.497
'cause I told them, I was like,

00:12:31.497 --> 00:12:32.330
(speaks in foreign language).

00:12:32.330 --> 00:12:34.637
And then they were, they made
me fill out a form in English,

00:12:34.637 --> 00:12:36.110
and I was like fuck, how do I tell them,

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:38.160
I have this blood condition?

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:39.677
And so, I Googled the kanji for it,

00:12:39.677 --> 00:12:42.140
and I like wrote this kanji,
which is fucking hard kanji,

00:12:42.140 --> 00:12:43.210
there was like swishes,

00:12:43.210 --> 00:12:46.560
when there's not lines,
straight lines, I'm like fuck!

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:47.393
(Garnt laughing)

00:12:47.393 --> 00:12:48.544
- There's a curve in this, damn it!

00:12:48.544 --> 00:12:49.377
(laughing)

00:12:49.377 --> 00:12:50.727
- And then I had to write this

00:12:50.727 --> 00:12:51.997
and it must have looked like

00:12:51.997 --> 00:12:53.327
a two-year-old wrote this kanji-

00:12:53.327 --> 00:12:54.254
(laughing)

00:12:54.254 --> 00:12:55.555
- I really wanna see
that now, just to see.

00:12:55.555 --> 00:12:56.941
- I don't know, whatever
the Kanji for hemophilia

00:12:56.941 --> 00:12:58.011
is on the screen right now.

00:12:58.011 --> 00:12:58.844
- Right.

00:12:58.844 --> 00:13:00.111
- Yeah, it was, and so I gave it,

00:13:00.111 --> 00:13:02.092
and I was like I made sure, I was like,

00:13:02.092 --> 00:13:03.120
(speaks in foreign language),

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:03.953
and they're like,

00:13:03.953 --> 00:13:04.892
(speaks in foreign language).

00:13:04.892 --> 00:13:06.510
And then they gave me the medicine,

00:13:06.510 --> 00:13:09.650
and then they gave me a
printed A4 paper in English

00:13:09.650 --> 00:13:11.280
explaining what all the medicine was,

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:12.975
and why I should take it.

00:13:12.975 --> 00:13:14.490
And I'm like, "Oh, wow, that's cool."

00:13:14.490 --> 00:13:15.667
- Yeah.

00:13:15.667 --> 00:13:17.107
- 'Cause normally in
Japan, it's just like,

00:13:17.107 --> 00:13:18.390
oh, you don't speak Japanese?

00:13:18.390 --> 00:13:19.223
- Good luck.
- Yeah, good luck.

00:13:19.223 --> 00:13:20.509
- Well shit, I think
this medicine will work.

00:13:20.509 --> 00:13:21.342
(laughing)

00:13:21.342 --> 00:13:22.955
You might have a reaction to it.

00:13:22.955 --> 00:13:24.616
- Riperoni, kid!

00:13:24.616 --> 00:13:25.449
(laughing)

00:13:25.449 --> 00:13:26.748
- Like better find out.

00:13:26.748 --> 00:13:27.581
(laughing)

00:13:27.581 --> 00:13:30.497
'Cause like most American
medicine I can't take,

00:13:30.497 --> 00:13:32.020
and I always find that out.

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:33.792
'Cause when I was a kid,
I didn't give a fuck.

00:13:33.792 --> 00:13:35.097
I was like, yeah, I'll take it.

00:13:35.097 --> 00:13:36.127
(laughing)

00:13:36.127 --> 00:13:36.963
(indictinct)

00:13:36.963 --> 00:13:38.580
- Is this a pill off a offer?

00:13:38.580 --> 00:13:39.630
What does it do?

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:40.463
- I'll give it a go.

00:13:40.463 --> 00:13:42.450
- Yeah, when I was 12, I
was like, oh it's medicine?

00:13:42.450 --> 00:13:44.080
Yeah I'll take it, like
it's good, it must be good.

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:46.593
And then when I got
older and I started like,

00:13:46.593 --> 00:13:48.970
when someone told me,
you can't take ibuprofen

00:13:48.970 --> 00:13:51.580
and my dad had been giving
me ibuprofen for five years.

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:52.563
- Oh, my God.

00:13:52.563 --> 00:13:53.950
- And it was like oh shit,
I could have like died.

00:13:53.950 --> 00:13:54.860
- Yeah.

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:56.490
- Yeah, so I can't take ibuprofen,

00:13:56.490 --> 00:13:57.960
and there's so many drugs in America,

00:13:57.960 --> 00:13:59.410
that contains stuff
that I just can't have.

00:13:59.410 --> 00:14:01.640
So, I can't have those generic ones.

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:04.030
- As a kid, did you love
the taste of Calpol?

00:14:04.030 --> 00:14:05.577
I think that was American Calpol.

00:14:05.577 --> 00:14:06.413
- I fucking love calpol.
- Oh, my God.

00:14:07.574 --> 00:14:08.635
- It's like an orange kind of thing?

00:14:08.635 --> 00:14:09.961
- It's like there was an orange Calpol,

00:14:09.961 --> 00:14:11.642
and a black currant one as well.

00:14:11.642 --> 00:14:13.260
- The black currant-

00:14:13.260 --> 00:14:14.311
- Was that like cough
medicine or something?

00:14:14.311 --> 00:14:15.144
- No it's kind of general cold medicine.

00:14:15.144 --> 00:14:16.180
- Yeah, just like general cold medicine,

00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:18.477
but as a kid, it tasted
way to good for it--

00:14:18.477 --> 00:14:20.292
- I used to love getting sick.

00:14:20.292 --> 00:14:21.125
(laughing)

00:14:21.125 --> 00:14:23.050
- Every time I would have a sick day,

00:14:23.050 --> 00:14:25.496
I'm like mom, bring out the Calpol.

00:14:25.496 --> 00:14:26.640
(laughing)

00:14:26.640 --> 00:14:27.658
- Cracking bottles.

00:14:27.658 --> 00:14:29.861
- Get the top shelf, and get the Calpol.

00:14:29.861 --> 00:14:34.533
- 'Cause you're only supposed
to take it like one spoonful,

00:14:34.533 --> 00:14:36.317
but sometimes I just act like extra ill,

00:14:36.317 --> 00:14:39.004
